          "role": "user",
          "content": "Board: Arduino Nano 33 BLE Sense, Arduino mbed core\n\n<handout>\n# Lab 4: Debounced button toggle (mock handout)\n\n> Mock handout written for testing `grader make-plan`. It is not an official course\n> document.\n\n## Goal\n\nUse a push button to toggle an LED, and report each change over a serial port.\n\n## Hardware\n\n* A push button between pin **D2** and GND. There is no external resistor, so use the\n  internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`). The button reads LOW while pressed.\n* The on-board LED on pin **D13**.\n* **Serial1** (pins D0/D1), not the USB `Serial` port, at **9600 baud**.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. The LED is **off** after reset.\n2. Each press of the button toggles the LED: the first press turns it on, the next one\n   turns it off, and so on.\n3. The LED must react **quickly**: a user should not notice any delay.\n4. Real buttons bounce. One press must toggle the LED exactly **once**, no matter how\n   much the contacts bounce.\n5. On every toggle, print `ON` or `OFF` (the new state) on its own line on Serial1.\n6. Implement debouncing in software, without blocking `delay()` calls.\n7. Comment your code.\n\n## Submission\n\nSubmit your sketch folder.\n</handout>\n\n<requirements>\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"led_off_after_reset\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The on-board LED (pin D13) is off immediately after the board resets or powers up.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"button_toggles_led\",\n    \"requirement\": \"Each press of the button on pin D2 toggles the LED: the first press turns it on, the next press turns it off, and so on.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"led_reacts_quickly\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The LED changes state within 20 ms of a valid button press, so the user notices no delay.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"debounce_single_toggle\",\n    \"requirement\": \"A single physical button press toggles the LED exactly once, even though the contacts bounce.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"serial_print_state\",\n    \"requirement\": \"On every toggle, the board prints the new LED state, either ON or OFF, on its own line over Serial1 at 9600 baud.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"uses_serial1_not_usb\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The code uses the Serial1 object (pins D0/D1) configured at 9600 baud for the state messages, not the USB Serial port.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pin_d2_input_pullup\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The code configures pin D2 as INPUT_PULLUP to read the button, since there is no external resistor.\"\n  }\n]\n</requirements>\n\n<student_values>\nnone\n</student_values>\n\n<student_code untrusted=\"true\">\n=== submission_04.ino ===\n   1| // button toggle w/ software debounce\n   2| // D2 button (pullup, LOW = pressed), D13 LED, Serial1 9600\n   3| \n   4| int buttonPin = 2;\n   5| int ledPin = 13;\n   6| \n   7| int ledState = LOW;\n   8| int buttonState = HIGH;        // debounced button\n   9| int lastReading = HIGH;        // previous raw reading\n  10| unsigned long lastBounce = 0;  // time of the last raw change\n  11| unsigned long debounceMs = 6;\n  12| \n  13| void setup() {\n  14|   pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);\n  15|   pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);\n  16|   digitalWrite(ledPin, ledState);\n  17|   Serial1.begin(9600);\n  18| }\n  19| \n  20| void loop() {\n  21|   int reading = digitalRead(buttonPin);\n  22| \n  23|   // raw input changed -> remember when\n  24|   if (reading != lastReading)\n  25|     lastReading = reading;\n  26|     lastBounce = millis();\n  27| \n  28|   // only act after the input has been steady for debounceMs\n  29|   if ((millis() - lastBounce) >= debounceMs) {\n  30|     if (reading != buttonState) {\n  31|       buttonState = reading;\n  32| \n  33|       if (buttonState == LOW) {\n  34|         ledState = !ledState;\n  35|         digitalWrite(ledPin, ledState);\n  36|         Serial1.println(ledState == HIGH ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\");\n  37|       }\n  38|     }\n  39|   }\n  40| }\n</student_code>"
